---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Eca from '../../components/Eca.astro';
import EcaTable from '../../components/EcaTable.astro';
import EcaFigure from '../../components/EcaFigure.astro';
const demo = (p: string) => `/demos/eca/?${p}#eca`;
---
<Base title="Elementary cellular automata" description="An interactive elementary cellular automaton: any Wolfram rule 0–255, periodic or zero boundaries, with a walkthrough of rule 90 for a crowd that plays it with their fingers." wide>
  <div class="measure">
    <h1>Elementary cellular automata</h1>
    <p>
      A row of cells, each either on or off. Time runs in ticks. At every tick each cell looks at three
      things only: its own state and the states of its left and right neighbour. A fixed rule says what
      it becomes next, and every cell switches at the same moment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three cells make 8 possible neighbourhoods, and a rule assigns an outcome to each, so there are
      2<sup>8</sup> = 256 rules, numbered 0 to 255. In the picture below the top row is the starting
      state and every row underneath is the next tick: the image is a history, not a snapshot. Click the
      table under the controls to flip one outcome and see the rule number change.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Eca rule={90} width={31} />

  <div class="measure">
    <p class="meta">
      Keys once the demo has focus (click it or go fullscreen): space plays and pauses, → or PageDown
      steps, ← or PageUp undoes a round, r resets, f toggles fullscreen. A presentation clicker sends
      PageDown and PageUp. The address bar follows the settings, so a set-up can be bookmarked.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rule-90">Rule 90</h2>
    <EcaTable rule={90} fingers />
    <p>
      Read the eight outcomes from case 111 down to case 000 and you get the binary
      number <code>01011010</code>, which is 90 in decimal. That is where every rule gets its number.
    </p>
    <p>
      Now look at what the table actually says: the outcome is 1 exactly when the left and right
      neighbours differ. Your own state plays no part. In crowd terms: <strong>look left, look right,
      ignore your own hand. If exactly one of your two neighbours has a finger up, put yours up for
      the next round. If both or neither do, put it down.</strong> Mathematically the new state is
      left XOR right, the sum of the two neighbours modulo 2.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start from a single finger up in the middle and rule 90 draws the Sierpinski triangle: the
      pattern spreads one seat per round in both directions, and the inside keeps hollowing out into
      smaller copies of the whole. Row <em>t</em> is row <em>t</em> of Pascal's triangle with the odd
      entries marked, because adding modulo 2 is exactly what the rule does.
    </p>

    <h2 id="boundaries">Boundaries</h2>
    <p>
      On a computer the row is usually a ring: the last cell counts the first as its right neighbour.
      In a room the row ends at the aisle, so the person on the end has one real neighbour and an empty
      seat. The simplest fix is a <em>phantom seat</em> on each end whose finger is always down. Those
      are zero boundary conditions, and they change the picture. The end person only copies their one
      real neighbour, since 0 XOR <em>x</em> = <em>x</em>.
    </p>
  </div>
  <ul class="grid">
    <li>
      <EcaFigure rule={90} width={15} gens={8} boundary="zero" label="Rule 90 on 15 seats with a phantom seat at each end, from one seed: the triangle grows for 7 rounds and row 8 is empty">
        15 seats, phantom seat at each end. Round 7 is every other finger up; round 8 is empty and
        stays empty. <a href={demo('rule=90&width=15&boundary=zero')}>Load it in the demo.</a>
      </EcaFigure>
    </li>
    <li>
      <EcaFigure rule={90} width={16} gens={8} boundary="periodic" label="Rule 90 on a ring of 16 from one seed: the two arms meet and row 8 is empty">
        A ring of 16. The two arms of the triangle meet on the far side and cancel: round 8 is empty
        too. <a href={demo('rule=90&width=16')}>Load it in the demo.</a>
      </EcaFigure>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <div class="measure">
    <p>
      That is no accident. With 2<sup><em>k</em></sup> − 1 seats and phantoms, or a ring of
      2<sup><em>k</em></sup> seats, a single seed dies out at round 2<sup><em>k</em>−1</sup>: at that
      moment the only two live cells of the infinite triangle land exactly on the phantom seats (or on
      the same cell of the ring, once from each side), and all that is left inside is zeros. Pick any
      other count and nothing dies. <a href={demo('rule=90&width=15')}>A ring of 15</a> repeats every 15
      rounds; <a href={demo('rule=90&width=20&boundary=zero')}>20 seats with phantoms</a> keep going and
      only repeat after 126 rounds. Whether the room ends in silence or in a loop depends on how many
      people are in the row.
    </p>

    <h2 id="crowd">Running it with a seated crowd</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Use one row of seats. An odd number is easiest: the middle person is the seed and starts with a
        finger up, everyone else down. Count the row and enter that number as cells, with zero boundary.</li>
      <li>The people at both ends have an empty chair, or you, beside them as the phantom seat. Its finger is
        always down.</li>
      <li>Each round: look at both neighbours, not at your own hand, and decide. Nobody moves yet. On
        "three", everyone changes at once. Deciding and changing have to be separate steps, or the quick
        ones will contaminate the round for the slow ones.</li>
      <li>Step the demo after every round as the answer key. Set the projector to fullscreen and use a
        clicker: PageDown steps, PageUp takes a round back. Click the demo first so the keys reach it.</li>
      <li>With 15 people the whole thing ends at round 8 with every finger down. With 16 people who close
        the ring by having the two end people watch each other, the same happens. With 20 people and
        phantoms it goes on until you stop it.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</Base>
